invariant: INV-14
file: srv_parser.c
catches: proof/prove-one.sh srv_parser_walk
reason: the parser counts the extensions after the duplicate check rather
  than before it, so srv_ext_duplicate, whose cost is the square of the
  count, runs over a block of any length before the bound refuses it
  (docs/decisions.md 59). Both checks answer illegal_parameter, so no
  alert shows the order and no test here can see it; only the work can.
  proof/srv_parser_walk_harness.c takes SRV_CLIENT_HELLO_EXT_MAX at 4 and
  proof/run.sh bounds the duplicate check's loops at four extensions, so
  an unwinding assertion fails once either loop runs over a fifth. Each run is a
  proof of minutes, so this belongs to the nightly's
  test-invariants-proof-backed job, never the fast tier.

--- old
    if (srv_ext_over_max(exts, exts_len)) {
        return srv_refuse(alert, ALERT_ILLEGAL_PARAMETER);
    }
    // One extension of each type per block (rfc9846.txt:1673-1674);
    // illegal_parameter is this design's choice under §6
    // (rfc9846.txt:3789-3791). add_frozen_extensions needs it as well.
    if (srv_ext_duplicate(exts, exts_len)) {
        return srv_refuse(alert, ALERT_ILLEGAL_PARAMETER);
    }
--- new
    // One extension of each type per block (rfc9846.txt:1673-1674);
    // illegal_parameter is this design's choice under §6
    // (rfc9846.txt:3789-3791). add_frozen_extensions needs it as well.
    if (srv_ext_duplicate(exts, exts_len)) {
        return srv_refuse(alert, ALERT_ILLEGAL_PARAMETER);
    }
    if (srv_ext_over_max(exts, exts_len)) {
        return srv_refuse(alert, ALERT_ILLEGAL_PARAMETER);
    }
